   115	## 2. Desk consequences and a serious termination attempt
   116	
   117	### 2.1 The correction really eliminates `D1`
   118	
   119	Direct collection, with every prime equal to `d/dZ`, gives (`review:219-250`)
   120	
   121	```text
   122	[A^4]Even = -2D2 E1-4D1 E2+10D2 E2'-8D2' E2
   123	 +Z(8D1 E2'+12D2 E1'-8D1' E2-4D2' E1).
   124	```
   125	
   126	Put `F=D1-D2'` and `G=E1-E2'`. After `E2=bZ eta^2` and
   127	`D2=aZ eta^3`, this is `2Z eta` times
   128	
   129	```text
   130	3a eta(2Z G' eta-G eta-2Z G eta')
   131	-2b(2Z F' eta-F eta-4Z F eta').
   132	```
   133	
   134	For `K=2bF-3a eta G`, its vanishing is equivalent to
   135	
   136	```text
   137	2Z eta K'-eta K-4Z eta' K=0.
   138	```
   139	
   140	If `K!=0`, `deg K=k` and `deg eta=e`, the coefficient at degree `e+k` is
   141	`eta_e K_k(2k-1-4e)`. The parenthesis is odd and cannot vanish. Hence `K=0`
   142	and
   143	
   144	```text
   145	2bF=3a eta G,   D1=D2'+(3a/(2b))eta G.
   146	```
   147	
   148	This is already a termination-by-degree argument, but only for the auxiliary
   149	`K`; it does not terminate the seven-polynomial system.
